  "response": "`crimes_00023` is the only `singular_plural_type_mismatch` finding for `src/api/state.ts`. It flags two declarations. In both cases the type and the value it's set to agree with each other, so the type is what the code means and the name is the mistake. I recommend renaming both, but the names the detector suggests are already taken in the same file, so they need different names.\n\nThe finding's evidence, verbatim:\n\n> - \"plural name, singular type: `users: User` @L24\"\n> - \"singular name, array type: `invoice: Invoice[]` @L27\"\n> - \"v1 detector — type aliases and generic types are silently skipped\"\n\nThe detector's `fix_shape` is: \"make the name agree with the type — `users` for array, `user` for scalar\".\n\n## 1. `users: User` (`src/api/state.ts:24`)\n\n```ts\nexport const users: User = { id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" };\n```\n\n**The type carries the meaning.** The value is one object literal with one `id`, so it really is a single `User`. Changing the type to `User[]` would mean wrapping the value in `[...]`, which turns one record into a list of one. That's a change in what the value is, not a naming cleanup.\n\n**Proposal: rename (a).** The obvious rename, `user`, is already used at L33 (`export const user: User = { id: \"u_2\", ... }`), so it would cause a redeclaration error. Pick a name that says which user this is, for example:\n\n```ts\nexport const seedUser: User = { id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" };\n```\n\n## 2. `invoice: Invoice[]` (`src/api/state.ts:27`)\n\n```ts\nexport const invoice: Invoice[] = [];\n```\n\n**The type carries the meaning.** The value `[]` is an empty list. A single `Invoice` can't be empty: it needs `id` and `totalCents`, and there's no sensible default for them. So the array type is what's intended.\n\n**Proposal: rename (a).** `invoices` is also taken, at L34 (`export const invoices: Invoice[] = [];`). L27 and L34 are now exactly the same, so the simplest fix is to **delete L27** and use `invoices`. If a second list is really needed, give it a name that says how it differs, such as `pendingInvoices: Invoice[]`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- **Nothing else uses these.** No file imports `src/api/state.ts`. The `invoice` matches in `src/billing.ts` (L40, L168) are separate local variables. Renaming affects only this file.\n- **These look intentional.** The file's header says it's \"Intentionally bad … fixture material\", and the comments on L23/L26 name this detector. L33–34 are labelled \"Negative controls — should NOT fire\". If the file exists to test the detector, the better fix is to leave the code alone and add `users` and `invoice` to `detectors.options.singular_plural_type_mismatch.allowedNames`, as the finding suggests. Renaming them would make the detector's own test stop firing.\n- **One point on contract drift.** `crime_00011` and `crime_00012` say that `User` and `Invoice` here are cut-down copies of the versions in `src/billing.ts`. \"Fix the type\" here should mean the name's array-vs-single shape only. It shouldn't be confused with merging those two definitions, which is separate work.\n\nI haven't edited anything, since you asked for proposals. If you tell me whether this file should stay as a test fixture, I'll either do the renames or add the `allowedNames` entries.",
